{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Trust in HaShem with all your heart, and lean not upon your understanding (Proverbs 3:5).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king admonishes in this verse (Proverbs 3) concerning the trait of bitachon (trust), because it is among the fundamental principles of the Torah and the commandments. He says that a person should trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, with all his heart, and not trust or lean upon his own understanding. The intention of this is that if his intellect instructs him and his understanding teaches him to trust in his wealth or in his strength and might, he should not lean on this at all, but rather place all his trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, who turns the wise backward and makes their knowledge foolish, and all his deeds and thoughts are nothing unless HaShem, may He be exalted, decrees it. And this is what is written (Proverbs 19:21): ״רבות מחשבות בלב איש ועצת ה' היא תקום״ — many thoughts are in a man's heart, but the counsel of HaShem, it shall stand. For if he has amassed abundant wealth, he has no cause to ascribe merit to himself, for his wealth did not come to him by his own strength and swiftness; and so Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 9:11): ״כי לא לקלים המרוץ ולא לגבורים המלחמה וגם לא לחכמים לחם וגם לא לנבונים עשר וגם לא ליודעים חן כי עת ופגע יקרה את כלם״ — the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the mighty, nor bread to the wise, nor riches to men of understanding, nor favor to men of skill, but time and chance happen to them all. And so he further said (Proverbs 16:1): ״לאדם מערכי לב ומה' מענה לשון״ — the preparations of the heart are man's, but the answer of the tongue is from HaShem. His speech is not in his own hand but in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and all the more so all other deeds; therefore it is not fitting for him to trust and lean upon his own understanding, nor to hang his hope upon his exertion, but only upon the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 62:6): ״אך לאלהים דומי נפשי כי ממנו תקותי״ — only to Elohim is my soul silent, for from Him is my hope. That is to say: not from my exertion." "<b>And know</b> that a person's trust is divided into eight levels. <b>The first</b> is at the beginning of his birth: he trusts upon his mother's breasts, because milk is his nourishment and life, as it is written (Psalms 22:10): ״מבטיחי על שדי אמי״. And <b>the second</b> is that afterward he trusts in his mother to feed him what pleases him and to give him the sweet to his taste, as David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 131:2): ״אם לא שויתי ודוממתי נפשי כגמול עלי אמו״. David, peace be upon him, says that he likened and compared his soul in its trust in HaShem, may He be blessed, to a weaned child that trusts upon its mother. <b>The third</b> is that he trusts in his father: for after he grows and the eyes of his intellect open a little, he sees that he and his mother depend upon his father, and his father is the mainstay, and then he hangs his trust upon his father. <b>The fourth</b> is that he trusts in his own strength: when he grows more and learns some craft by which he can earn a living, he then removes his trust from his father and places his trust in his own strength and devices. <b>The fifth</b> is that he trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He: when he grows more and his intellect strengthens, and through seeing that his own strength and devices are nothing, and he sees the deficiency of creatures and their lowliness and their great need for His kindnesses, may He be exalted, he then removes the trust he had in his own strength and places his trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, in matters over which he has no power and device to procure. Such as the need for rain at the time of sowing; or if he travels by sea and the sea grows stormy; or in a desert of beasts and robbers; or in a settled land and there is a plague in the land. And all these matters and their like, over which he has no power or device to escape, he places his trust in the Holy One, blessed be He. And this level in the trait of bitachon is good, but it lacks perfection, because it is as the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Jeremiah 2:27): ״ובעת רעתם יאמרו קומה והושיענו״ — and in the time of their trouble they will say, Arise and save us. <b>The sixth</b> is that he trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He, even in matters over which he does have power and some device — such as one who could earn a living by means of difficult labor that fatigues the body, yet turns back his trust to the Holy One, blessed be He, that He provide for him in a manner that is easier. <b>The seventh</b> is that he trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He, in all causes altogether, whether the cause is difficult or easy. <b>The eighth</b> is that he trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He, with all his heart, and does not attach his thought to any cause among causes, but rather desires whatever is the will of the Holy One, blessed be He — that is to say, whether he is healthy or sick, rich or poor, free or imprisoned in the house of imprisonment. And since the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, is that one of those occurrences or afflictions befall him and He desires to crush him through them, he should desire this, and he should not seek a cause; for if he exerts himself and seeks a cause to escape from that occurrence that is upon him, he sets one portion of his heart upon trusting that cause and does not trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, with complete trust in all his heart. And whoever is perfect in the trait of bitachon is obliged to trust in the Holy One, blessed be He, with all his heart, that He, may He be exalted, who is the master of all causes, will provide him with causes according to what he needs. Thus wrote the great and pious rabbi R. Bahya ibn Paquda, of blessed memory, in the book Ḥovot HaLevavot that he composed." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that because of this he said (Proverbs 3) ״בטח אל ה'״ — trust toward (el) HaShem — and did not say בטח בה' (trust in HaShem), to explain that complete trust in all one's heart is that all his thoughts be directed toward (el) HaShem, as it is written (Psalms 25:15): ״עיני תמיד אל ה'״ — my eyes are ever toward HaShem. And it is not fitting for the righteous man who is perfect in the trait of bitachon to seek a cause; and the proof of this is the matter of Elijah, as I mentioned in the preceding parashah (1 Kings 17:6): ״והעורבים מביאים לו לחם ובשר בבקר״ — and the ravens brought him bread and meat in the morning, etc. And the Holy One, blessed be He, provided him with that cause, and he did not seek it. Behold, this is a very exalted level in the trait of bitachon." "<b>And now,</b> Joseph the righteous, who trusted in the Holy One, blessed be He — because he asked the chief butler to be a cause for him to get out of prison, therefore he did not reach this level of bitachon and was punished for this by remaining two more years, in punishment for the two words he said to him: ״והזכרתני והוצאתני״ — ״and remember me, and get me out.״ And this is what is written:" "<b>And it came to pass at the end of two full years, and Pharaoh dreamed, and behold, he stood by the river — at the end of two full years.</b> At the end of two years after Joseph interpreted the dream of the chief butler and the chief baker, this dream of Pharaoh occurred. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted that his detention in the prison house for two years beyond that was a punishment for 'and you shall mention me' and 'and you shall bring me out.' And I have already explained above that Joseph the righteous placed his trust in HaShem alone, and did not turn to the chief butler except that he might be for him a cause from HaShem, blessed be He. And since it already mentioned 'two years,' why was 'days' (ימים) necessary? For it is written (Genesis 45:6): ״כי זה שנתים הרעב״ — 'for these two years has the famine been' — and it did not mention 'two full years.' Rather, it teaches you here that they were two complete years, day for day; therefore it added ימים." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'At the end of two full years' — this is what the verse says (Job 28:3): ״קץ שם לחשך״ — 'He set an end to darkness.' Everything that issues from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, comes forth with a fixed measure — even the rains come down with a fixed measure. He apportions to the creatures on Rosh HaShanah: when Israel merits, they descend upon the vegetation and upon the trees, and when they sin, they descend upon the deserts and upon the seas. This is what the verse says: ״קץ שם לחשך״ — He set an end to darkness. He set an end for the exodus from Egypt, as it says (Exodus 12:41): ״ויהי מקץ שלשים שנה וארבע מאות שנה״ — 'And it came to pass at the end of thirty years and four hundred years,' etc. He set an end for Joseph to go forth from the prison house, as it says: ״ויהי מקץ שנתים ימים״ — 'And it came to pass at the end of two full years.'" "<b>And Pharaoh dreamed.</b> It would have been fitting to say 'and Pharaoh king of Egypt,' but because this dream concerned the river, and it was the beginning of his downfall, and it also contains an allusion to what is destined to befall him through water, therefore Scripture did not mention kingship in connection with it, but his name alone. And because of this you will find that throughout the entire portion Scripture always mentions the name Pharaoh, and you will never find there 'king of Egypt' at all — except when the elevation of Joseph is involved, where Scripture mentions kingship in honor of Joseph; that is what is written (Genesis 41): ״ויוסף בן שלשים שנה בעמדו לפני פרעה מלך מצרים״ — 'And Joseph was thirty years old when he stood before Pharaoh king of Egypt.' For there it mentioned both his name and his kingship, to make known that the merit of Joseph was the cause of his kingship, for the entire land would have been lost were it not for his counsel in the wisdom of his interpretation." "<b>And know</b> that dreams come about for three reasons: because of foods, because of thoughts, and because of the strength of the soul. Foods produce coarseness and raise vapor to the brain, and a dream that comes about for this reason is all matters of vanity and idle things. A dream that comes about because of thoughts is when a person thinks about certain things constantly, and from the force of the mental image that he conjures of them by day, he sees them at night; this is what is written concerning Nebuchadnezzar (Daniel 2:29): ״רעיוניך על משכבך סליקו״ — 'your thoughts arose upon your bed.' And the dream that comes about because of the strength of the soul is the true one, the most worthy of all, which derives from the powers of the soul: the imaginative faculty depicts, during sleep, the perceived things that it had already considered and grasped while awake and engraved in its imagination; and at the time of sleep, when the senses are suspended, this faculty grows strong, until the matter appears to him as though he perceived it in actual sensation. And this dream comes true when the imaginative faculty is strong in a person, and it is the kind that comes to a person without his having pondered it, and not in the customary manner at all. And this is the minor prophecy of which our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that it was given to fools and to children, because they have no intellect and did not exert their senses while awake to grasp perceived matters and to retain them in their imagination; therefore, whatever their imaginative faculty accomplishes for them while awake, it accomplishes for them during sleep — they imagine true and enduring things. And dreams of this kind come both to the righteous and to the wicked, without a person pondering the matter at all. And the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to heal Pharaoh and to move his spirit with a dream, and to confound the counsel of his magicians and his wise men, in order to elevate Joseph to that high station." "<b>And behold, he stood by the river.</b> יאור (ye'or) and נהר (nahar) are one and the same, and so Onkelos translated it נהרא (nahara). And the root of both of them is from the word אורה (light), and so we find that Scripture called the rains אור, as it says (Job 36:32): ״על כפים כסה אור״, and it is written (Job 37:11): ״יפיץ ענן אורו״. And the reason for this is that rivers are made from rainwater, and the rains come about through the luminaries, and therefore it is fitting to attribute the offspring to the fathers." "<b>And the river.</b> This river is called the Nile, which would rise and water the land, for rains do not fall regularly in Egypt. And what it stated — 'upon the river' — it should have said 'on the bank of the river,' as it says, ״ותעמדנה אצל הפרות על שפת היאור״, and likewise Pharaoh himself, in his account to Joseph, said, ״בחלומי הנני עומד על שפת היאור״. <b>But</b> it is possible to explain that since this is the Torah's narrative, the Torah revealed to us here his evil thought, for his dream was of the nature of his thinking — that he made himself a god, and the Nile was his river; and because of this it said 'upon the river': he saw in his dream that he was lord over the river and ruled over it, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 29:3): ״לי יארי ואני עשיתיני״. And Pharaoh, in his account, changed the matter and said 'on the bank of the river,' because he was bewildered by the dream, and that is what is written, ״ותפעם רוחו״; and he was ashamed to claim greatness for himself by saying 'upon the river,' so he said 'on the bank of the river.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:57.583472544Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}